      "question": "How did Qualcomm's customer-concentration and customer-designed-chip risks change from FY2024 to FY2025? Give me a concise risk brief that distinguishes a stronger warning from a new exposure, then connect the comparison to relevant later developments involving major handset customers. Make clear which effects are reported facts and which remain expectations.",
      "answer": "- **Bottom line:** FY2025 strengthens Qualcomm’s warnings about existing customer dependence and customer-designed chips; it does not establish a genuinely new economic exposure. Comparison: original 10-Ks for FY2024 (September 25, 2023–September 29, 2024; filed November 6, 2024) and FY2025 (September 30, 2024–September 28, 2025; filed November 5, 2025). [S1][S2]\n\n- **Customer concentration—more explicit downside, not a new dependency.** Both years warn that a few customers and premium handsets dominate revenue, giving customers purchasing leverage and making order losses or delays consequential. FY2025 adds an explicit expectation that increasing Apple internal-modem use **“will have a significant negative impact”** on QCT revenue, operating results and cash flows. It also limits the Apple market-share/mix warning to Apple devices using Qualcomm modems. These changes sharpen the mechanism and expected severity; they do not demonstrate that concentration increased. [S3][S4]\n\n- **Customer-designed chips—stronger Apple warning and more specific examples.** FY2024 already expected Apple to replace Qualcomm modems in some or all future devices, described Samsung’s internal chips, and warned about Chinese OEM substitution. FY2025 names Apple, Samsung and Xiaomi together and explicitly emphasizes increasing Apple substitution and significant QCT harm. Xiaomi’s inclusion is newly specific wording, not evidence that Chinese-customer vertical integration first became a risk in FY2025. [S5][S6]\n\n- **Post-filing Apple developments—deployment versus expectations.**\n  - On September 20, 2026, BigGo reported Apple C2 use in some iPhone 18 models while a US iPhone 18 Pro Max teardown retained a Qualcomm modem. This supports reported, partial substitution—not a completed Apple exit or an all-clear for Qualcomm. [W2]\n  - TechTimes’ July 30, 2026 report attributed to CEO Cristiano Amon an expectation of roughly halved Apple-related chip revenue between the September and December 2026 quarters. That was a **prospective company expectation**, not an already realized loss. [W1]\n\n- **Keep reported effects separate.** TechTimes’ September 1, 2026 report linked reduced handset-chip purchases and prior-generation Snapdragon selection to higher memory costs. That reported purchasing pressure fits the concentration warning, but does **not** establish that Apple, Samsung or Xiaomi internal chips caused the decline. Returned Samsung/Xiaomi commentary did not date their underlying actions sufficiently to establish a distinct post-filing substitution event. [W8][W6]",
